Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to recreate a
Python script with the following specification: Create a single-file Python 3
command-line script intended for Termux on Android (use the shebang
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`).
The script recursively compiles C and C++ source files found under the current
working directory, in parallel, and reports the results.
Requirements: **Imports and dependencies** - Use only the Python standard
library: `from __future__ import annotations`, `subprocess`, `sys`,
`multiprocessing.Pool`, and `pathlib.Path`. **Top-level worker function (must be
a module-level function so it can be pickled by multiprocessing)** - Name it
`compile_file` and have it accept a single tuple argument containing: the source
file path (a `Path`), the compiler command name (a string), and the desired
output path (a `Path`). - Build the command as: compiler, then the source path
as a string, then `-o`, then the output path as a string. - Run it with
`subprocess.run`, capturing stdout and stderr as text, with a 60-second timeout.
- On return code 0, return a tuple of (source path as string, `True`, and a
success message of the form "✓ Compiled: <source file name> -> <output file
name>"). - On a non-zero return code, return (source path as string, `False`,
and a failure message of the form "✗ Failed: <source file name>" followed by a
newline and the captured stderr). - Catch `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` and return
a failure tuple with the message "✗ Timeout: <source file name>". - Catch any
other `Exception` and return a failure tuple with the message "✗ Error: <source
file name> - <exception text>". - Every path returns the same 3-element tuple
shape: (path string, success boolean, message string). **`main` function** - Set
the root directory to the current working directory (`Path.cwd()`). - Print
"Scanning directory: <root path>" followed by a blank line. - Collect all `*.c`
files and all `*.cpp` files recursively using `rglob`, into separate lists. -
Build a task list: for each `.c` file, the task is (file, "clang", file with its
suffix removed); for each `.cpp` file, the task is (file, "clang++", file with
its suffix removed).
Process all C tasks before C++ tasks. - If there are no tasks, print a message
saying no `.c` or `.cpp` files were found, and exit with a nonzero status code
via `sys.exit`. - Otherwise, compile all tasks in parallel using a
`multiprocessing.Pool` (default worker count), via `imap`/`map`-style iteration
over the task list. - For each result, print its message.
Track the count of successes and failures. - After completion, print a summary
line showing the number of successful and failed compilations (for example,
"Done: X succeeded, Y failed"), and exit with status 1 if any failed, otherwise
exit normally (status 0). **Entry point** - Guard the call to `main()` with the
standard `if __name__ == "__main__":` check.
Use `multiprocessing` safe conventions so it works when spawning workers.
**Behavior notes and edge cases** - Output names are the source path with its
extension removed, in the same directory as the source file; existing output
files will be overwritten by the compiler. - Files are discovered recursively,
so nested directories are included. - Compiler availability is not checked up
front; a missing compiler surfaces as a per-file "Error" message rather than
crashing the script. - Stderr is included in the output only for failed
compilations. - Output should be written to stdout using the exact symbols (✓
and ✗) and formatting described above. - Keep the code concise, idiomatic, with
minimal comments and no external dependencies. ---
